<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna of Bohemia and Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was the daughter of King Vladislaus II and sister of Louis II, and the wife of Archduke Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the future Emperor Ferdinand I, whom she married in 1521 under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>–Jagiellonian double marriage treaty. When Louis II died at Mohács in 1526, her rights supported Ferdinand's claim to the crowns of Bohemia and Hungary, founding the Habsburg Monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. She bore fifteen children.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-of-bohemia-and-hungary.jpg" alt="Anna of Bohemia and Hungary" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Maler zu Schwaz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Maler_-_Queen_Anne_of_Hungary_and_Bohemia_-_WGA13895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
